  "body": "Browsers currently have an inconsistent behavior when dragging a `<div draggable=\"true\">` element which does not add any data to its DataTransfer object. \r\n\r\nIn Gecko [bug 1352852](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1352852#c7), I did some testing with [this jsfiddle](https://jsfiddle.net/g5w82mos/1/), and got the following results:\r\n\r\n* Chrome and Edge created a ghost image of the node when the node was picked up, and successfully dropped it onto the drop zone, firing a drop event.\r\n* Safari and IE created the ghost image, but dropping it onto the drop zone did not fire a drop event.\r\n* Firefox did not create a ghost image, and dropping onto the drop zone did not fire a drop event.\r\n\r\nIn bug 1352852 I've written some patches to bring Firefox's behavior in line with Chrome and Edge's, as it appears to be the behavior implied by the spec, but perhaps we should clarify this edge case in the spec.",
